   section 'Notable people':
      {{see also|:Category:People from Hunan}}
      During the late [[Qing dynasty|late Qing Era]], Hunan emerged as the epicenter of revolution and reform in China, giving rise to many of the nation's most renowned scholars, politicians, and military leaders, including the most influential and controversial fi
      Hunanese were pioneers in embracing foreign ideas, as seen in the works of 19th-century thinkers like [[Wei Yuan]] (魏源). As the saying goes among Chinese historians: "Hunanese shaped half of modern Chinese history" (一部中国近代史，半部由湖南写就). Their profound and endurin
      * [[Zhou Dunyi]] (1017–1073), Chinese scholar and philosopher
      * [[Wang Fuzhi]] (1619–1692), Chinese essayist, historian, and philosopher of the late Ming, early Qing dynasties.
      * [[Zeng Guofan]] (1811–1872)
      * [[Zuo Zongtang]] (1812–1885), or General Tso, Chinese statesman and military leader of the late Qing dynasty.
      * [[Taqibu]] (1816–1855)
      * [[Huang Xing]] (1874–1916)
      * [[Cai E]] (1882–1916), Chinese revolutionary leader, General and Governor of [[Yunnan]] (1911–1913)
